{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Hear, O Israel: YHWH our Elohim, YHWH is one.</b> Rashi explained: He is our Elohim and not the Elohim of the nations, but in the future YHWH will be one. The explanation of the matter is as Rabbi Aḥa bar Ḥanina said, for it is written: ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא יִהְיֶה ה׳ אֶחָד״ (Zechariah 14:9) — is it then that nowadays He is not one? Rather, the world to come is not like this world: for in this world, over good tidings one says ״בָּרוּךְ הַטּוֹב וְהַמֵּטִיב״, and over bad tidings one says ״בָּרוּךְ דַּיַּן הָאֱמֶת״, but in the world to come over all of them they will say ״הַטּוֹב וְהַמֵּטִיב״ (Pesachim 50a). For in this world, inasmuch as one sees acts of reversal — to harm or to benefit — therefore some went out into heresy, saying that there are two powers, for from one source two opposites would not emerge, as explained above in parashat Bereshit (Genesis 1:5) on the verse ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר יוֹם אֶחָד״. But in the world to come, when all the troubles have already been forgotten, and all praise and say over good tidings ״הַטּוֹב וְהַמֵּטִיב״, and they will not see the reversal then — therefore YHWH will be one in the mouth of all, and His name one. For the change of names from judgment to mercy causes them this error; therefore ״ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ״ — that is, the name of judgment and the name of mercy which Israel alone ascribe to one El — and in the future it will be ״ה׳ אֶחָד״, for all of them will use none but the name of mercy." "<b>YHWH is one.</b> In the word ״שְׁמַע״ the ע is large, and in the word ״אֶחָד״ the ד is large — a hint to what they said in the Midrash (Tosefot Ḥagigah 3a, s.v. מי כעמך): that the Holy One, blessed be He, and Israel testify one about the other, etc., as it is written (Isaiah 43:10): ״אַתֶּם עֵדַי נְאֻם ה׳״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said that the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses the knot of tefillin (Berakhot 7a). And the matter of this knot is as he concludes there (ibid.): the tefillin of the Master of the Universe — what is written in them? etc. — and he concludes there that written in them is: ״אֶת ה׳ הֶאֱמַרְתָּ הַיּוֹם וַה׳ הֶאֱמִירְךָ הַיּוֹם״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: You made Me a single unity in the world — ״שְׁמַע יִשְׂרָאֵל ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ ה׳ אֶחָד״ — I too will make you a single unity in the world, etc. And this is the matter of the knot of tefillin: for just as we bind the parchment as a seal on heart and arm for the unification of the Name, blessed be He, so the Holy One, blessed be He, sets us as a seal on His heart and as a seal on His arm, to make us one nation on earth. And this is the bond between us and our Creator, blessed be He: that we both testify one about the other the matter of oneness, in the manner of one who comes to see — so he comes to be seen. And this matter is sound according to the plain sense." "However, according to the hint, it is possible to say, in the mystery of the large ע and ד and the matter of the knot of tefillin: for Moses requested and said: ״הַרְאֵנִי נָא אֶת כְּבוֹדֶךָ״ (Exodus 33:18), and they said in the Midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 45:5): he sought to see every reward of the righteous in this world and in the world to come, for there, ״שָׁם כָּבוֹד חֲכָמִים יִנְחָלוּ״. And He answered him: ״וְרָאִיתָ אֶת אֲחוֹרָי וּפָנַי לֹא יֵרָאוּ״ (ibid. 33:23), and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 7a): ״אֲחוֹרָי״ — this is the knot of tefillin. For in the tefillin there is the name Shaddai, namely the ד in the knot of the tefillin at the back, and the letters יוּ״ד שִׁי״ן form the word יֵ״שׁ at the front — that is, ש in the head-tefillin and י in the arm-tefillin — and יֵ״שׁ alludes to the reward of the world to come, as it is said (Proverbs 8:21): ״לְהַנְחִיל אֹהֲבַי יֵ״שׁ״. For in it there is substance and reality, and by comparison the reward of this world is reckoned as nothingness and void, and this is hinted at in the ד, for its increase comes from all four directions of the world, as it is written (Genesis 28:14): ״וּפָרַצְתָּ יָמָּה וָקֵדְמָה״ etc. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: ״וְרָאִיתָ אֶת אֲחוֹרָי״ — the ד in the knot of tefillin, hinting at the reward of this world, that alone you will be able to see; and it is a secondary thing called 'the back.' And so He said to Moses: ״עֲלֵה רֹאשׁ הַפִּסְגָּה וְשָׂא עֵינֶיךָ יָמָּה צָפֹנָה וָנֶגְבָּה״ etc., to show him all His 'back' parts, as Rashi explained in parashat VeZot HaBerakhah (Deuteronomy 34:1–2): ״וַיַּרְאֵהוּ אֶת כָּל הָאָרֶץ״ etc., ״עַד הַיָּם הָאַחֲרוֹן״ — and not inclusive, for from the last sea onward is the time of the beginning of the reward of the world to come, and concerning it is it said (Isaiah 64:3): ״עַיִן לֹא רָאָתָה אֱלֹהִים זוּלָתְךָ״. Therefore it says: ״וּפָנַי לֹא יֵרָאוּ״ — for on the front of the tefillin the word יֵ״שׁ is engraved, hinting at the reward of the world to come, which eye has not seen. And this is the mystery of the large ע and ד — to say that the ע of a man cannot see anything but the ד as stated, but יֵ״שׁ is hinted at at the head of the word of ״שְׁמַע יִשְׂרָאֵל״: for the יֵ״שׁ hinting at the inheritance of His beloved — יֵ״שׁ — the eye cannot see; rather only let the ear hear — ״שְׁמַע״ — this promise from the mouth of scribes and enumerators, but the eye has not seen. And this is a precious hint." "Another matter: therefore the large ע and ד came, because it is said in Job (24:8–9): ״הֵן קֶדֶם אֶהֱלֹךְ וְאֵינֶנּוּ וְאָחוֹר וְלֹא אָבִין, שְׂמֹאל בַּעֲשֹׂתוֹ וְלֹא אָחַז יַעְטֹף יָמִין וְלֹא אֶרְאֶה״. And all this is a proof of His oneness, blessed be He, that He is not seen in any place to the exclusion of another place — if so, He is not corporeal. And since the seeing eye, in all four directions, cannot see Him from there, because He is not a divisible body — this is a proof of His oneness, blessed be He."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The Kli Yakar reads יֵ״שׁ (yod-shin) as both the literal Hebrew word for 'substance/being' and as an acronym formed by the letters yod (arm tefillin) and shin (head tefillin), which together spell the name Shaddai along with the dalet (the knot). The wordplay עין-דלת and שמע-ישׁ is central and preserved throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:00.248532192Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}